@@ -0,0 +1,63 @@
+---
+title: "Configuring Vulnerability Intelligence"
+description: "Keeping a local copy of the CISA KEV catalog and FIRST EPSS scores"
+---
+
+Ghostwriter can keep a local copy of two public sources of vulnerability intelligence, so the CVE IDs on your findings can be checked against them:
+
+* **CISA's Known Exploited Vulnerabilities (KEV) catalog**: vulnerabilities CISA knows are exploited in the wild, with the date each was added, the remediation due date for US federal agencies, the required action, and whether it is known to be used in ransomware campaigns.
+* **FIRST's Exploit Prediction Scoring System (EPSS)**: for each CVE, the probability of exploitation activity in the next 30 days, and its percentile among all scored CVEs. Scores change daily.
+
+Both sources are off by default. Ghostwriter makes no requests to them until an administrator enables one.
+
+<Info>
+This feature is part of the benheise/Ghostwriter fork and its patches. It is not in upstream Ghostwriter.
+</Info>
+
+## Enabling the Sources
+
+Open the admin panel and go to **Vulnerability Intelligence > Vulnerability Intelligence Configuration**.
+
+| Setting | Default | What it does |
+|---------|---------|--------------|
+| Download the CISA KEV Catalog | Off | Keeps the whole catalog, about 1,800 entries. Entries CISA removes are kept and marked with the date they disappeared |
+| Download EPSS Scores | Off | Keeps scores only for the CVE IDs your library and report findings reference, so the table stays small |
+| Update Frequency | Daily | Disabled (refresh manually), every 6 hours, every 12 hours, daily, or weekly. EPSS publishes once a day, so refreshing more often only helps KEV |
+| Run Time (UTC) | 14:00 | When daily and weekly refreshes run, and where the 6- and 12-hour cycle starts. FIRST publishes each day's scores at about 13:30 UTC |
+
+Saving the page creates, changes, or removes a Django Q schedule named **Refresh Vulnerability Intelligence**. The page shows the next refresh time. The configuration page is authoritative: if you edit that schedule in the Django Q admin, the next save of the configuration replaces your edit. If someone deletes the schedule, the page says so, and saving it restores the schedule.
+
+Use **Refresh Now** at the top of the page to queue a refresh immediately. The **Status** section shows when each source last updated, the KEV catalog version, the EPSS score date and model version, how many entries are stored, and the errors from the last run.
+
+When EPSS is enabled and a finding gains a CVE ID that has no score yet, Ghostwriter asks FIRST's EPSS API for just that CVE in the background, so the score appears without waiting for the next refresh. Refreshes always use FIRST's daily file rather than the API, as FIRST asks.
+
+## How Refreshes Protect Your Data
+
+A refresh never replaces good data with bad data:
+
+* Downloads use HTTPS with certificate verification, honour the server's proxy settings, and stop at the **Request Timeout** and **Maximum Download Size** limits. The size limit also applies to the decompressed EPSS file.
+* A catalog with fewer than 1,000 usable entries, or a scores file with fewer than 100,000 rows, is treated as truncated and rejected.
+* A KEV catalog released before the stored one, or EPSS scores dated before the stored ones, are rejected.
+* Each source runs separately. If one fails, the other still updates, and the failure appears under **Last Errors**.
+
+## Restricted Networks
+
+If the server cannot reach CISA or FIRST, point the feed URLs at an internal mirror (they must use `https://`), or load files you copy in yourself:
+
+```bash
+docker compose -f production.yml run --rm django python manage.py import_kev /path/to/known_exploited_vulnerabilities.json
+docker compose -f production.yml run --rm django python manage.py import_epss /path/to/epss_scores-YYYY-MM-DD.csv.gz
+```
+
+Both commands apply the same checks as a scheduled refresh and work whether or not the sources are enabled. `import_epss` keeps only the scores for CVE IDs your findings reference. To run a refresh from the command line instead of the admin panel, use `python manage.py refresh_vuln_intel`.
+
+<Note>
+The refresh job is listed in `GHOSTWRITER_DJANGO_Q_SCHEDULE_TASKS`, and the EPSS lookup in `GHOSTWRITER_DJANGO_Q_INTERNAL_TASKS`. If a settings file in `production.d` replaces either dictionary, add those entries back or the jobs will be refused.
+</Note>
+
+## Where the Data Is Available
+
+* **Admin panel:** read-only, searchable lists of KEV entries and EPSS scores.
+* **GraphQL API:** the `knownExploitedVulnerability` and `epssScore` queries, for the user and manager roles. Look entries up by the finding's `cves`, for example `knownExploitedVulnerability(where: {cveId: {_in: $cves}})`. Service tokens do not have access to these tables.
+
+KEV data is published by CISA. EPSS is free to use, and FIRST asks that you credit it (first.org/epss) wherever you show the scores.
